<tamination title="Box the Gnat">

  <tam title="Box the Gnat"
       from="Facing Couples"
       formation="Facing Couples Compact"
       sequencer="gender-specific">
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripleft" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1="1" cx2="2" cy2="1" x2="2.5" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="-2" x4="0" y4="-2" />
    </path>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripright" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1=".1" cx2="2" cy2=".1" x2="2.5" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="2" x4="0" y4="2" />
    </path>
  </tam>

  <tam title="Box the Gnat"
       from="Right-Hand Wave"
       formation="Wave RH"
       sequencer="gender-specific">
    <path>
      <move select="U-Turn Right" hands="gripright" offsetX="1" offsetY="-2"/>
    </path>
    <path>
      <move select="U-Turn Left" hands="gripright" offsetX="1"/>
    </path>
  </tam>

  <!-- The variant the published get-outs need, and the data did not have.
       The two tams above are authored for the couples THREE (Facing Couples Compact, ms) and FOUR
       (Facing Couples, b2) apart. Every board the corpus reaches `Box the Gnat` from is an Eight
       Chain Thru or a Trade By, whose facing couples are TWO apart, so both missed - and a miss is
       not a near-miss here, because matching compares distances: the measured best errors were
       2.000 and 4.000 against the 1.5 tolerance, which is why the call was the top entry in the
       report's "exists but will not match" list (7 published lines).

       The motion is the same couple turn; only the travel scales with the separation, and the two
       authorings pin that relation: `x2` is 3 at separation 4 and 2.5 at separation 3, i.e.
       `x2 = separation/2 + 1`, so separation 2 gives `x2 = 2`. Everything else is unchanged, and
       the start formation is written INLINE at x = -1 so the engine's own mirror (180 degrees
       about the origin) puts the second couple at x = +1 - the separation 2 the boards use. -->
  <tam title="Box the Gnat"
       from="Facing Couples"
       sequencer="gender-specific">
    <formation>
      <dancer gender="boy" x="-1" y="1" angle="0" />
      <dancer gender="girl" x="-1" y="-1" angle="0" />
    </formation>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripleft" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1="1" cx2="2" cy2="1" x2="2" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="-2" x4="0" y4="-2" />
    </path>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripright" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1=".1" cx2="2" cy2=".1" x2="2" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="2" x4="0" y4="2" />
    </path>
  </tam>
  <!-- SEPARATION SIX, the board `Heads Box the Gnat` is asked for. The heads of a Static Square are
       two couples facing each other SIX apart (the north couple at x = +-1, y = +3 and the south at
       x = +-1, y = -3), and the three authorings above are separations 3, 4 and 2, so all four
       figures that call `Heads Box the Gnat` from a squared set refuse.

       The extrapolation is the one the separation-2 authoring states: `x2 = separation / 2 + 1`, so
       separation 6 gives `x2 = 4`, and the inline formation is at x = -3 so the engine's own mirror
       puts the second couple at x = +3. `x2` is the only number that changes - the rest of the
       couple turn is the same motion, exactly as it is across the 3 and 4 authorings.

       THIS IS THE EXPERIMENT PHASE 16b REVERTED ONCE, and it is being re-tried rather than
       re-derived because the two cases are not the same: `Star Thru` at separation 6 collapsed all
       eight dancers onto ONE spot (both boxes' centres coincide), so it was taken back out. Here the
       four facing pairs of a Static Square meet at four DIFFERENT points - the heads' at (1,0) and
       (-1,0), the sides' at (0,1) and (0,-1) - so the collision that decided that case does not
       arise. The revert's stated cost was five get-out successes, so BOTH the census and the
       get-out harnesses decide this one, and it comes straight back out if they fall. -->
  <tam title="Box the Gnat"
       from="Facing Couples"
       sequencer="gender-specific">
    <formation>
      <dancer gender="boy" x="-3" y="1" angle="0" />
      <dancer gender="girl" x="-3" y="-1" angle="0" />
    </formation>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripleft" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1="1" cx2="2" cy2="1" x2="4" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="-2" x4="0" y4="-2" />
    </path>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripright" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1=".1" cx2="2" cy2=".1" x2="4" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="2" x4="0" y4="2" />
    </path>
  </tam>


  <!-- MIGRATED from the retired Basic levels when `b1`/`b2` were removed in favour of
       `ms` 2026. These are the (title, formation, from) combinations that existed ONLY under the
       Basic levels; every other Basic tam is already re-authored here. -->
<tam title="Box the Gnat"
       from="Facing Couples"
       formation="Facing Couples"
       sequencer="gender-specific">
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripleft" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1="1" cx2="2" cy2="1" x2="3" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="-2" x4="0" y4="-2" />
    </path>
    <path>
      <movement hands="gripright" beats="4" cx1="1" cy1=".1" cx2="2" cy2=".1" x2="3" y2="0" cx3="1.3" cx4="1.3" cy4="2" x4="0" y4="2" />
    </path>
  </tam>

</tamination>
